 "genre_note": "163 notes over 20 documents (39,375 words); 98.8% of words assigned corpus-wide, median note 205 source words, largest 1,006 (tech_layoffs_november_2023) \u2014 every note well inside the 1,800-word cap, so no split was forced by density alone.\n\nBB distribution: empirical_observation 90, concept 41, argument 13, model 9, counter_argument 8, hypothesis 1, procedure 1. This matches the genre expectation \u2014 tech news is overwhelmingly dated events plus entity descriptions. I did not manufacture the rare blocks. `hypothesis` occurs exactly once and it is genuine: doc_0048 blocks 17-20 state the \"winter break hypothesis\" \u2014 a falsifiable prediction that GPT-4 degrades in December because it \"learned\" to do less work over the holidays, with OpenAI confirming the behaviour but not the cause. `procedure` occurs once (doc_0603 14-16, Daye's ordered send-kit \u2192 self-swab \u2192 post-to-lab \u2192 digital-result \u2192 optional \u00a329 nurse consult workflow). `model` was reserved for real causal/relational mechanisms, not summaries: the 10^25 FLOPs threshold as the classification mechanism for systemic-risk GPAIs, Meta's forced legal-basis chain (contractual necessity rejected \u2192 legitimate interests quashed by CJEU \u2192 consent), Apple's chip-naming vs. thermal-envelope explanation for why an M2 Ultra beats an M3, Beeper's BPNs workaround for Android lacking native APNs, and tampon-vs-swab sample-collection surface area.\n\nThree roundup documents drove the shape of the plan. doc_0049 (2023 tech layoffs, 483 blocks, 200+ companies) is one document but hundreds of unrelated items; splitting by month gives 12 topically coherent notes (each = one month of the layoff wave) plus an overview, all 157-1,006 words. doc_0048 (ChatGPT explainer, 298 blocks) was decomposed thematically rather than by month, because a \"November 2023\" note would mix a DDoS outage, GPT-4 Turbo and a Pew poll; the 24 resulting notes split across three sub-plans (product/platform, safety & regulation, reference FAQ). doc_0584 (Verge deals) was split by product category.\n\nEntity sharing: eight notes draw blocks from more than one document. The Altman firing note pulls from doc_0516, doc_0415 and doc_0048; the reinstatement, negotiation and Altman's-next-move notes each merge doc_0415 with doc_0048; the EU AI Act two-tier GPAI rules, entry-into-force timeline, AI Office and political-agreement notes merge doc_0564 with doc_0525; the X disinformation and DSA notes merge doc_0396 with doc_0248. No (doc, block) pair is assigned twice \u2014 verified programmatically.\n\nCorrections were inspected rather than discarded, and two contradict their own headlines, so both are assigned: doc_0488 block 20 states Spill's seed extension is $2 million, not the $2.7M in the headline (assigned to spill_seed_extension_funding_round); doc_0540 block 27 corrects Beeper Mini's price to $1.99/mo, not $2.99 (assigned to beeper_product_lineup_pricing_and_availability). By contrast doc_0564 block 51 is a genuine no-op update notice listing additions already present in the body, and was dropped.\n\nCoverage is \u226594% on 19 of 20 documents. The one exception is doc_0584 at 88.4%: the Verge anti-Prime Day roundup is structurally promotional \u2014 11 \"The best X deals\" category headers, three of which (\"smart speaker\", \"Mesh router\", \"Miscellaneous\") have no content at all, plus a Verge Deals X-follow plug and a newsletter signup block with reCAPTCHA boilerplate. 178 of its 1,531 words are chrome with no retrievable claim. Everything with an actual price or product fact is assigned."
